Section: The Field

 A field is its modulus in limbs together with the three values
 Montgomery multiplication needs. They are computed once per
 verification and threaded, rather than recomputed per operation:
 `cb-r-squared` doubles thirty-two times per limb, so recomputing it
 inside the point loop would cost more than the point loop.

 The curve's own constants ride on the field rather than being read from
 the top of the chapter. The field is already threaded through every
 function that needs them, so this adds no parameter anywhere, and it is
 what makes one copy of the arithmetic serve both curves. `ef-b`, `ef-gx`
 and `ef-gy` are empty for a field built over the group order, which
 never sees the curve equation.

  EcField = record {
    ef-m : List Integer,
    ef-n : Integer,
    ef-n0 : Integer,
    ef-r2 : List Integer,
    ef-one : List Integer,
    ef-w : Integer,
    ef-mb : List Integer,
    ef-e2 : List Integer,
    ef-b : List Integer,
    ef-gx : List Integer,
    ef-gy : List Integer
  }

  ec-field : List Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> EcField
  ec-field (m-bytes) (limbs) (e2) (b) (gx) (gy) =
    ec-field-of (cb-from-bytes m-bytes limbs) limbs m-bytes e2 b gx gy

  ec-field-of : List Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> EcField
  ec-field-of (m) (limbs) (mb) (e2) (b) (gx) (gy) =
    EcField {
      ef-m = m,
      ef-n = limbs,
      ef-n0 = cb-n0inv m,
      ef-r2 = cb-r-squared m limbs,
      ef-one = cb-one limbs,
      ef-w = limbs * 2,
      ef-mb = mb,
      ef-e2 = e2,
      ef-b = b,
      ef-gx = gx,
      ef-gy = gy
    }

 Multiplication is Montgomery's, so both operands and the result are in
 Montgomery form and the form is never mixed. Addition and subtraction
 need no conversion at all: x maps to xR, and that map is linear, so a
 sum of Montgomery forms is the Montgomery form of the sum.

  ec-mul-f : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-mul-f (a) (b) (f) = cb-mont-mul a b f.ef-m f.ef-n f.ef-n0

  ec-to-mont : List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-to-mont (a) (f) = cb-mont-mul a f.ef-r2 f.ef-m f.ef-n f.ef-n0

  ec-from-mont : List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-from-mont (a) (f) = cb-mont-mul a f.ef-one f.ef-m f.ef-n f.ef-n0

 Every operation returns a fresh list. `cb-take` copies, and the carry
 walks then mutate only that copy, so no caller's value is ever written
 through. This matters more than it looks: `cb-sub-into` mutates its
 first argument in place, and a point formula reuses its inputs several
 times, so an operation that subtracted into a caller's coordinate would
 corrupt the next line rather than this one.

  ec-add-f : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-add-f (a) (b) (f) = ec-add-carry (cb-take a f.ef-n) b f

  ec-add-carry : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-add-carry (r) (b) (f) = ec-add-reduce r (ec-addc r b f.ef-n 0 0) f

 A sum that overflowed the top limb is above the modulus however the low
 limbs compare, so the carry is tested before the comparison rather than
 instead of it.

  ec-add-reduce : List Integer, Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-add-reduce (r) (c) (f) =
    if c > 0 then cb-sub-into r f.ef-m f.ef-n
    else if cb-cmp r f.ef-m f.ef-n >= 0 then cb-sub-into r f.ef-m f.ef-n
    else r

  ec-addc : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ec-addc (r) (b) (n) (i) (carry) =
    if i >= n then carry
    else ec-addc-step r b n i ((list-at r i) + (list-at b i) + carry)

  ec-addc-step : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ec-addc-step (r) (b) (n) (i) (v) =
    ec-addc (list-set-at r i (bit-and v cb-mask)) b n (i + 1) (bit-shru v cb-shift)

 Subtraction that goes negative wraps by adding the modulus back. The
 borrow out of the top limb is exactly the test for that, and it is
 returned rather than clamped for the same reason CryptoBig's subtraction
 carries its borrow explicitly.

  ec-sub-f : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-sub-f (a) (b) (f) = ec-sub-borrow (cb-take a f.ef-n) b f

  ec-sub-borrow : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-sub-borrow (r) (b) (f) = ec-sub-reduce r (ec-subb r b f.ef-n 0 0) f

  ec-sub-reduce : List Integer, Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-sub-reduce (r) (bw) (f) =
    if bw == 0 then r
    else ec-wrap-done r (ec-addc r f.ef-m f.ef-n 0 0)

  ec-wrap-done : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-wrap-done (r) (c) = r

  ec-subb : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ec-subb (r) (b) (n) (i) (borrow) =
    if i >= n then borrow
    else ec-subb-step r b n i ((list-at r i) - (list-at b i) - borrow)

  ec-subb-step : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ec-subb-step (r) (b) (n) (i) (d) =
    if d < 0 then ec-subb (list-set-at r i (d + cb-base)) b n (i + 1) 1
    else ec-subb (list-set-at r i d) b n (i + 1) 0

  ec-dbl-f : List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-dbl-f (a) (f) = ec-add-f a a f

  ec-is-zero : List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ec-is-zero (a) (n) = ec-zero-loop a n 0

  ec-zero-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ec-zero-loop (a) (n) (i) =
    if i >= n then True
    else if list-at a i /= 0 then False
    else ec-zero-loop a n (i + 1)

 Inversion by Fermat: a^(p-2) is a^-1 for every non-zero a modulo a
 prime. `cb-mod-exp-bytes` works on plain byte strings rather than
 Montgomery limbs, so the value leaves Montgomery form on the way in and
 returns to it on the way out. That is two conversions for one inversion,
 and it happens once per verification, not once per point.

  ec-inv-mont : List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-inv-mont (a) (f) =
    let plain = cb-to-bytes (ec-from-mont a f) f.ef-w
    in let inv = cb-mod-exp-bytes plain f.ef-e2 f.ef-mb
    in ec-to-mont (cb-from-bytes inv f.ef-n) f

Section: Points

 Jacobian coordinates: (X, Y, Z) stands for the affine point
 (X/Z^2, Y/Z^3). The whole reason for them is that affine addition needs
 an inversion and inversion here costs a full modular exponentiation, so
 an affine scalar multiplication would perform about three hundred and
 eighty of them. In Jacobian coordinates the entire scalar multiplication
 needs none, and the single inversion is paid at the end.

 The point at infinity is Z = 0. Its X and Y are never read, because
 every operation tests Z first.

  EcPoint = record {
    ep-x : List Integer,
    ep-y : List Integer,
    ep-z : List Integer
  }

  ec-infinity : EcField -> EcPoint
  ec-infinity (f) =
    EcPoint { ep-x = cb-one f.ef-n, ep-y = cb-one f.ef-n, ep-z = cb-zeros f.ef-n }

  ec-is-inf : EcPoint, EcField -> Boolean
  ec-is-inf (p) (f) = ec-is-zero p.ep-z f.ef-n

  ec-point-of : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> EcPoint
  ec-point-of (xb) (yb) (f) =
    EcPoint {
      ep-x = ec-to-mont (cb-from-bytes xb f.ef-n) f,
      ep-y = ec-to-mont (cb-from-bytes yb f.ef-n) f,
      ep-z = ec-to-mont (cb-one f.ef-n) f
    }

 Doubling, the a = -3 form. alpha is 3(X - Z^2)(X + Z^2), which is
 3X^2 - 3Z^4 expanded so that it costs one multiplication rather than
 two squarings and a subtraction. This is where the curve's choice of a
 is spent.

  ec-double : EcPoint, EcField -> EcPoint
  ec-double (p) (f) =
    if ec-is-inf p f then p
    else ec-dbl-go p f

  ec-dbl-go : EcPoint, EcField -> EcPoint
  ec-dbl-go (p) (f) =
    let delta = ec-mul-f p.ep-z p.ep-z f
    in let gamma = ec-mul-f p.ep-y p.ep-y f
    in let beta = ec-mul-f p.ep-x gamma f
    in let cross = ec-mul-f (ec-sub-f p.ep-x delta f) (ec-add-f p.ep-x delta f) f
    in let alpha = ec-add-f (ec-dbl-f cross f) cross f
    in ec-dbl-fin p f delta gamma beta alpha

  ec-dbl-fin : EcPoint, EcField, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> EcPoint
  ec-dbl-fin (p) (f) (delta) (gamma) (beta) (alpha) =
    let beta4 = ec-dbl-f (ec-dbl-f beta f) f
    in let x3 = ec-sub-f (ec-mul-f alpha alpha f) (ec-dbl-f beta4 f) f
    in let yz = ec-add-f p.ep-y p.ep-z f
    in let z3 = ec-sub-f (ec-sub-f (ec-mul-f yz yz f) gamma f) delta f
    in let g8 = ec-dbl-f (ec-dbl-f (ec-dbl-f (ec-mul-f gamma gamma f) f) f) f
    in let y3 = ec-sub-f (ec-mul-f alpha (ec-sub-f beta4 x3 f) f) g8 f
    in EcPoint { ep-x = x3, ep-y = y3, ep-z = z3 }

 Addition of two Jacobian points. The intermediates are gathered into a
 record rather than threaded as parameters because the formula needs six
 of them alive at once and a nine-parameter helper is harder to read than
 the arithmetic it carries. The record costs one allocation against the
 eleven multiplications around it.

  EcAddTmp = record {
    at-z1z1 : List Integer,
    at-z2z2 : List Integer,
    at-u1 : List Integer,
    at-h : List Integer,
    at-s1 : List Integer,
    at-r : List Integer
  }

 The degenerate cases are the whole reason this is not one formula.
 H = 0 means the two points share an affine x. If r is also zero they are
 the same point, and the addition formula divides by zero there, so
 doubling is the answer. If r is not zero they are a point and its
 negation, and the sum is infinity. Leaving either case to the general
 formula returns a plausible point that is not on the curve.

  ec-add-pt : EcPoint, EcPoint, EcField -> EcPoint
  ec-add-pt (p) (q) (f) =
    if ec-is-inf p f then q
    else if ec-is-inf q f then p
    else ec-add-disp p q f (ec-add-tmp p q f)

  ec-add-tmp : EcPoint, EcPoint, EcField -> EcAddTmp
  ec-add-tmp (p) (q) (f) =
    let z1z1 = ec-mul-f p.ep-z p.ep-z f
    in let z2z2 = ec-mul-f q.ep-z q.ep-z f
    in let u1 = ec-mul-f p.ep-x z2z2 f
    in let u2 = ec-mul-f q.ep-x z1z1 f
    in let s1 = ec-mul-f p.ep-y (ec-mul-f q.ep-z z2z2 f) f
    in let s2 = ec-mul-f q.ep-y (ec-mul-f p.ep-z z1z1 f) f
    in EcAddTmp {
         at-z1z1 = z1z1, at-z2z2 = z2z2, at-u1 = u1,
         at-h = ec-sub-f u2 u1 f, at-s1 = s1, at-r = ec-sub-f s2 s1 f
       }

  ec-add-disp : EcPoint, EcPoint, EcField, EcAddTmp -> EcPoint
  ec-add-disp (p) (q) (f) (t) =
    if ec-is-zero t.at-h f.ef-n == False then ec-add-main p q f t
    else if ec-is-zero t.at-r f.ef-n then ec-double p f
    else ec-infinity f

  ec-add-main : EcPoint, EcPoint, EcField, EcAddTmp -> EcPoint
  ec-add-main (p) (q) (f) (t) =
    let rr = ec-dbl-f t.at-r f
    in let ii = ec-mul-f (ec-dbl-f t.at-h f) (ec-dbl-f t.at-h f) f
    in let jj = ec-mul-f t.at-h ii f
    in let vv = ec-mul-f t.at-u1 ii f
    in let x3 = ec-sub-f (ec-sub-f (ec-sub-f (ec-mul-f rr rr f) jj f) vv f) vv f
    in ec-add-fin p q f t rr jj vv x3

  ec-add-fin : EcPoint, EcPoint, EcField, EcAddTmp, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> EcPoint
  ec-add-fin (p) (q) (f) (t) (rr) (jj) (vv) (x3) =
    let sj = ec-mul-f t.at-s1 jj f
    in let y3 = ec-sub-f (ec-mul-f rr (ec-sub-f vv x3 f) f) (ec-dbl-f sj f) f
    in let zs = ec-add-f p.ep-z q.ep-z f
    in let zt = ec-sub-f (ec-sub-f (ec-mul-f zs zs f) t.at-z1z1 f) t.at-z2z2 f
    in EcPoint { ep-x = x3, ep-y = y3, ep-z = ec-mul-f zt t.at-h f }

 Double and add, most significant bit first, starting at the scalar's
 highest set bit. Starting above it would square in leading zeros, which
 is harmless and slow; a scalar of zero has no set bit and answers
 infinity, which is what `cb-top-bit` returning minus one arranges.

  ec-scalar-mul : List Integer, EcPoint, EcField -> EcPoint
  ec-scalar-mul (k) (p) (f) = ec-sm-loop k p f (ec-infinity f) (cb-top-bit k)

  ec-sm-loop : List Integer, EcPoint, EcField, EcPoint, Integer -> EcPoint
  ec-sm-loop (k) (p) (f) (acc) (i) =
    if i < 0 then acc
    else ec-sm-step k p f (ec-double acc f) i

  ec-sm-step : List Integer, EcPoint, EcField, EcPoint, Integer -> EcPoint
  ec-sm-step (k) (p) (f) (acc) (i) =
    if cb-bit-at k i == 1
    then ec-sm-loop k p f (ec-add-pt acc p f) (i - 1)
    else ec-sm-loop k p f acc (i - 1)

 The affine x coordinate, as thirty-two big-endian bytes, which is the
 form the signature's r is compared against. This is the one inversion
 the coordinate system was chosen to defer.

  ec-affine-x : EcPoint, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-affine-x (pt) (f) = ec-affine-with pt f (ec-inv-mont pt.ep-z f)

  ec-affine-with : EcPoint, EcField, List Integer -> List Integer
  ec-affine-with (pt) (f) (zi) =
    cb-to-bytes (ec-from-mont (ec-mul-f pt.ep-x (ec-mul-f zi zi f) f) f) f.ef-w

Section: Key Validation

 A public key is checked before it is used. Two independent things are
 required: each coordinate must be a field element, and the point must
 satisfy the curve equation.

 For ECDSA verification this is a smaller hazard than it is for key
 agreement, where a point on a different curve leaks the private scalar.
 It is done anyway because the cost is one squaring and two
 multiplications against the several thousand the verification already
 spends, and because a key that is not on the curve is a key whose
 signature check means nothing whatever it answers.

  ec-on-curve : EcPoint, EcField -> Boolean
  ec-on-curve (q) (f) =
    ec-limbs-eq (ec-mul-f q.ep-y q.ep-y f) (ec-rhs q f) f.ef-n

  ec-rhs : EcPoint, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-rhs (q) (f) =
    let x2 = ec-mul-f q.ep-x q.ep-x f
    in let x3 = ec-mul-f x2 q.ep-x f
    in let three-x = ec-add-f (ec-dbl-f q.ep-x f) q.ep-x f
    in ec-add-f (ec-sub-f x3 three-x f) (ec-to-mont (cb-from-bytes f.ef-b f.ef-n) f) f

  ec-limbs-eq : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ec-limbs-eq (a) (b) (n) = cb-cmp a b n == 0

Section: Verification

 SHA-256 answers eight thirty-two-bit WORDS and not thirty-two bytes.
 Handing the word list to something that wants a byte string produces
 output that is plausible and silently wrong, which is how a whole TLS
 key schedule once shipped in the wrong units. The conversion is
 big-endian and it happens here, once.

  ec-digest-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  ec-digest-bytes (msg) = ec-words-bytes (sha256 msg) 0 []

 SHA-384 answers six SIXTY-FOUR bit words where SHA-256 answers eight of
 thirty two, so the byte conversion is a different one and not a longer
 run of the same one. `Foreword chapter Rsa` already needed this shape for
 PKCS#1 and spells it `rsa-words64-bytes`.

  ec-digest384-bytes : List Integer -> List Integer
  ec-digest384-bytes (msg) = ec-words64-bytes (sha384 msg) 0 []

  ec-words64-bytes : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ec-words64-bytes (ws) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ws then acc
    else ec-words64-bytes ws (i + 1) (ec-push-word64 acc (list-at ws i))

  ec-push-word64 : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-push-word64 (acc) (w) =
    let a1 = list-push acc (bit-and (bit-shru w 56) 255)
    in let a2 = list-push a1 (bit-and (bit-shru w 48) 255)
    in let a3 = list-push a2 (bit-and (bit-shru w 40) 255)
    in let a4 = list-push a3 (bit-and (bit-shru w 32) 255)
    in let a5 = list-push a4 (bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255)
    in let a6 = list-push a5 (bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255)
    in let a7 = list-push a6 (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
    in list-push a7 (bit-and w 255)

  ec-words-bytes : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ec-words-bytes (ws) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= list-length ws then acc
    else ec-words-bytes ws (i + 1) (ec-push-word acc (list-at ws i))

  ec-push-word : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-push-word (acc) (w) =
    let a1 = list-push acc (bit-and (bit-shru w 24) 255)
    in let a2 = list-push a1 (bit-and (bit-shru w 16) 255)
    in let a3 = list-push a2 (bit-and (bit-shru w 8) 255)
    in list-push a3 (bit-and w 255)

 A digest is a full two hundred and fifty six bits and n is slightly
 below 2^256, so a digest can exceed the group order and must be reduced.
 Two hundred and fifty six bits is under twice n, so one conditional
 subtraction is the whole reduction. The same argument covers the x
 coordinate on the way out, where p is likewise above 2^255.

  ec-reduce-mod : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-reduce-mod (a) (m) (n) = ec-reduce-fix (cb-take a n) m n

  ec-reduce-fix : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-reduce-fix (r) (m) (n) =
    if cb-cmp r m n >= 0 then cb-sub-into r m n
    else r

 Multiplication modulo n of two plain values. Montgomery multiplication
 answers a*b*R^-1, so multiplying that by R^2 and reducing once more
 gives a*b with both operands and the result outside Montgomery form.
 Two reductions to avoid carrying a form through six lines is the right
 trade at this call count.

  ec-mulmod : List Integer, List Integer, EcField -> List Integer
  ec-mulmod (a) (b) (f) = ec-mul-f (ec-mul-f a b f) f.ef-r2 f

  ec-bytes-eq : List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  ec-bytes-eq (a) (b) =
    if list-length a /= list-length b then False
    else ec-bytes-loop a b 0

  ec-bytes-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ec-bytes-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if list-at a i /= list-at b i then False
    else ec-bytes-loop a b (i + 1)

  ec-bytes-lt : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ec-bytes-lt (a) (b) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then False
    else if list-at a i < list-at b i then True
    else if list-at a i > list-at b i then False
    else ec-bytes-lt a b (i + 1)

  ec-bytes-zero : List Integer -> Boolean
  ec-bytes-zero (a) = ec-bzero-loop a 0

  ec-bzero-loop : List Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  ec-bzero-loop (a) (i) =
    if i >= list-length a then True
    else if list-at a i /= 0 then False
    else ec-bzero-loop a (i + 1)

  ec-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-slice (bs) (off) (len) = ec-slice-loop bs off len 0 []

  ec-slice-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ec-slice-loop (bs) (off) (len) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length bs then acc
    else ec-slice-loop bs off len (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at bs (off + i)))

 Both signature components must lie in [1, n-1]. RFC 6090 and SEC1
 require the range check, and it is not decoration: r or s equal to zero
 sails through the arithmetic below and can be made to verify.

  ec-scalar-ok : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  ec-scalar-ok (v) (w) (nb) =
    if list-length v /= w then False
    else if ec-bytes-zero v then False
    else ec-bytes-lt v nb 0

 The public key is an uncompressed SEC1 point: 0x04 and then the two
 coordinates, sixty-five bytes. Compressed points are not accepted, and
 the reason is the same one the rest of this chapter gives for refusing
 things: decompressing requires a square root, a square root has two
 answers, and choosing between them from a parity bit is a decision this
 chapter would rather not make on a caller's behalf. Certificates in
 practice carry uncompressed points.

 The curve and the digest are independent choices and the pairing is the
 certificate's, not ours. A P-256 key signed under SHA-384 is the common
 case on the public web: the elliptic-curve intermediates of Sectigo and
 Let's Encrypt are exactly that, so refusing the combination refuses
 chains that never mention P-384.

  ecdsa-p256-verify : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p256-verify (pub) (msg) (sig) =
    ecdsa-verify pub (ec-digest-bytes msg) sig ec-p256-curve ec-p256-order

  ecdsa-p256-verify-sha384 : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p256-verify-sha384 (pub) (msg) (sig) =
    ecdsa-verify pub (ec-digest384-bytes msg) sig ec-p256-curve ec-p256-order

  ecdsa-p384-verify : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p384-verify (pub) (msg) (sig) =
    ecdsa-verify pub (ec-digest-bytes msg) sig ec-p384-curve ec-p384-order

  ecdsa-p384-verify-sha384 : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p384-verify-sha384 (pub) (msg) (sig) =
    ecdsa-verify pub (ec-digest384-bytes msg) sig ec-p384-curve ec-p384-order

  ec-p256-curve : EcField =
    ec-field ec-p-bytes ec-limbs ec-p-minus-2 ec-b-bytes ec-gx-bytes ec-gy-bytes

  ec-p256-order : EcField =
    ec-field ec-n-bytes ec-limbs ec-n-minus-2 [] [] []

  ec-p384-curve : EcField =
    ec-field ec384-p-bytes ec384-limbs ec384-p-minus-2 ec384-b-bytes ec384-gx-bytes ec384-gy-bytes

  ec-p384-order : EcField =
    ec-field ec384-n-bytes ec384-limbs ec384-n-minus-2 [] [] []

  ecdsa-verify : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, EcField, EcField -> Boolean
  ecdsa-verify (pub) (dg) (sig) (fp) (fn) =
    if list-length pub /= fp.ef-w * 2 + 1 then False
    else if list-at pub 0 /= 4 then False
    else if list-length sig /= fp.ef-w * 2 then False
    else ecdsa-scalars pub (ec-leftmost dg fn.ef-w) (ec-slice sig 0 fp.ef-w) (ec-slice sig fp.ef-w fp.ef-w) fp fn

 FIPS 186-4 section 6.4 takes z from the LEFTMOST min(N, outlen) bits of
 the digest, not from the digest reduced modulo n. The two agree whenever
 the digest is no longer than the order, which is why this was invisible
 until SHA-384 met P-256: a 48-byte digest against a 32-byte order has to
 lose its low sixteen bytes, and feeding the whole thing in produced a
 verifier that refused every genuine signature. Both curves here are a
 whole number of bytes wide, so the truncation is a slice and never a bit
 shift.

  ec-leftmost : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  ec-leftmost (dg) (w) =
    if list-length dg <= w then dg
    else ec-slice dg 0 w

  ecdsa-scalars : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, EcField, EcField -> Boolean
  ecdsa-scalars (pub) (dg) (r) (s) (fp) (fn) =
    if ec-scalar-ok r fp.ef-w fn.ef-mb == False then False
    else if ec-scalar-ok s fp.ef-w fn.ef-mb == False then False
    else ecdsa-key dg r s (ec-slice pub 1 fp.ef-w) (ec-slice pub (fp.ef-w + 1) fp.ef-w) fp fn

  ecdsa-key : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, EcField, EcField -> Boolean
  ecdsa-key (dg) (r) (s) (qx) (qy) (fp) (fn) =
    if ec-bytes-lt qx fp.ef-mb 0 == False then False
    else if ec-bytes-lt qy fp.ef-mb 0 == False then False
    else ecdsa-onq dg r s fp fn (ec-point-of qx qy fp)

  ecdsa-onq : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, EcField, EcField, EcPoint -> Boolean
  ecdsa-onq (dg) (r) (s) (fp) (fn) (q) =
    if ec-on-curve q fp == False then False
    else ecdsa-p256-us dg r s fp q fn

 s inverse modulo n, then u1 = z/s and u2 = r/s, both reduced to plain
 byte strings because the scalar multiplication reads bits off bytes.

  ecdsa-p256-us : List Integer, List Integer, List Integer, EcField, EcPoint, EcField -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p256-us (dg) (r) (s) (fp) (q) (fn) =
    let z = ec-reduce-mod (cb-from-bytes dg fn.ef-n) fn.ef-m fn.ef-n
    in let sinv = cb-from-bytes (cb-mod-exp-bytes s fn.ef-e2 fn.ef-mb) fn.ef-n
    in let u1 = cb-to-bytes (ec-mulmod z sinv fn) fn.ef-w
    in let u2 = cb-to-bytes (ec-mulmod (cb-from-bytes r fn.ef-n) sinv fn) fn.ef-w
    in ecdsa-p256-sum r fp fn u1 u2 q

  ecdsa-p256-sum : List Integer, EcField, EcField, List Integer, List Integer, EcPoint -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p256-sum (r) (fp) (fn) (u1) (u2) (q) =
    let g = ec-point-of fp.ef-gx fp.ef-gy fp
    in let a = ec-scalar-mul u1 g fp
    in let b = ec-scalar-mul u2 q fp
    in ecdsa-p256-final r fp fn (ec-add-pt a b fp)

 The signature verifies when the x coordinate of u1*G + u2*Q, reduced
 modulo n, equals r. An infinite sum is a failure and not a special
 value: it is the case where the two scalar multiples cancelled, and
 there is no x coordinate to compare.

  ecdsa-p256-final : List Integer, EcField, EcField, EcPoint -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p256-final (r) (fp) (fn) (pt) =
    if ec-is-inf pt fp then False
    else ecdsa-p256-cmp r fn (ec-affine-x pt fp)

  ecdsa-p256-cmp : List Integer, EcField, List Integer -> Boolean
  ecdsa-p256-cmp (r) (fn) (xb) =
    let x = ec-reduce-mod (cb-from-bytes xb fn.ef-n) fn.ef-m fn.ef-n
    in ec-bytes-eq (cb-to-bytes x fn.ef-w) r

